5. Mr. AJ Mohammad Ali, learned Advocate appearing for the petitioner submitted that admittedly the suit land appertaining to CS Plot No. 494 is 04 acres out of 3.36 acres which was recorded in CS Khatian as kandi land and not as chandina land and the plaintiffs father having had taken settlement of the land in 1351 BS (September, 1944) the settlement is governed by the Bengal Tenancy Act and not by the Transfer of Property Act. The lower appellate Court erred in holding that the land is chandina land and that the plaintiff failed to produce bilateral deed and that the settlement with regard to the suit land was not valid in law. Mr. AJ Mohammad Ali submitted that when settlement is made by the Zaminder in respect of an agricultural land governed by Bengal Tenancy Act, no document in writing is necessary for making such settlement and accordingly the learned Court of appeal below committed mistake in law leading to mistake in his decision and that has occasioned failure of justice. 

 8. In numerous decisions of the Court of this sub-continent it has been consistently held that settlement of agricultural land governed by Bengal Tenancy Act does not require any document, not to speak of bilateral document in writing, for creating settlement. If the landlord accepts premium and Tents for the land settled, the settlement is valid in law. Mr. AJ Mohammad Ali cited the decision in this respect given in the case of Hochen Mandai &amp; others vs. Miraj Ali Pramanik &amp; ors, 4 DLR 457 wherein it has been held that: 

 &quot;Mr. MH Khondker, the learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the plaintiff landlord, contends that under the Bengal Tenancy Act no document is necessary for the creation of any agricultural tenancy. It is no doubt true that if any party resorted to any document creating a permanent tenancy it must be registered but if the document is not registered the tenant can prove his tenancy independently of the written lease inasmuch as under the Bengal Tenancy Act no written lease is necessary for the creation of the tenancy.&quot; 

 &quot;Under the Bengal Tenancy Act no written document is necessary for the creation of a lease in respect of agricultural lands........................................&quot;
